<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-riemann-integrability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Riemann Integrability
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now explore the Riemann integrability of various functions and introduce the Riemann integral. We will see several equivalent characterizations of this property, expressed as conditions on limits of certain nets of Riemann sums and Darboux sums.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-riemann-integrability">
    <title>
      Riemann integrability
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a bounded real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>, and let <m> I \in \R </m> be a real number. The following are equivalent:
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For any positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
              
              <md>
                \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \lt \epsilon
              </md>
              
              for all tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> of mesh <m> \mesh \of{ P } \lt \delta </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The net <m> \mathcal{ R }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } }_{ \of{ P , t } } </m> of real numbers which associates to each tagged partition <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> the corresponding Riemann sum of <m> f </m> converges upwards to <m> I </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The nets <m> \mathcal{ U }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> and <m> \mathcal{ L }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> of real numbers which associate to each partition <m> P </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> the corresponding upper and lower Darboux sums of <m> f </m> converge upwards to <m> I </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> B = \sup_{ a \leq x \leq b }{ \abs{ f \of{ x } } } </m>. If <m> B = 0 </m>, then <m> f \of{ x } = 0 </m> for all <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>, and so each of the three above properties holds. Thus we may suppose for the remainder of the proof that <m> B \gt 0 </m>. First suppose that for any positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
              
              <md>
                \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \lt \epsilon
              </md>
              
              for all tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> of mesh <m> \mesh \of{ P } \lt \delta </m>. Let <m> N \in \Nbhd_{ \R } \of{ I } </m> be a neighborhood of <m> I </m> in <m> \R </m>, and note that <m> \mB_{ \epsilon } \of{ I } \subseteq N </m> for some positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. Choose a tagged partition <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m> of mesh <m> \mesh \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } \lt \delta </m>. Then for all refinements <m> \of{ P , t } \succeq \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m> of <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m>, <m> \mesh \of{ P } \leq \mesh \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } \lt \delta </m>, and so
              
              <md>
                \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \lt \epsilon
              </md>.
              
              We conclude that the net <m> \mathcal{ R }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } }_{ \of{ P , t } } </m> converges upwards to <m> I </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now suppose that the net <m> \mathcal{ R }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } }_{ \of{ P , t } } </m> converges upwards to <m> I </m>. Since the nets <m> \mathcal{ U }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> and <m> \mathcal{ L }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> are monotone and bounded by <xref ref="proposition-properties-of-darboux-sums"/>, they converge to real numbers
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            U \amp = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } } = \inf_{ P }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            L \amp = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } } = \sup_{ P }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        by a net analogue of the <xref ref="theorem-monotone-convergence-theorem" text="title"/>. So it suffices to show <m> I = U = L </m>. We first observe that for any partition <m> P </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, there is some tagged partition <m> \of{ Q , t } </m> so that <m> P \preceq Q </m> and <m> \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q , t } - I } \lt \epsilon </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } \leq \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ Q } \leq \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q , t } \lt I + \epsilon
        </md>,
        
        and
        
        <md>
          \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } \geq \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ Q } \geq \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q , t } \gt I - \epsilon
        </md>.
        
        Since this holds for all partitions <m> P </m> and all positive real numbers <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>,
        
        <md>
          L = \sup_{ P }{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } } \leq I \leq \inf_{ P }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } } = U
        </md>.
        
        It remains to show that <m> U \leq I \leq L </m>. We will show that <m> U \leq I </m>; the proof that <m> I \leq L </m> is similar.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        To that end, let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Then there is some tagged partition <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
        </md>
        
        for all refinements <m> \of{ P , t } \succeq \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , s_{ \epsilon } } </m>. In particular, by further refining <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , s_{ \epsilon } } </m> we may assume without loss of generality that it is left tagged. Let <m> P_{ \epsilon } = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m>. We aim to define a further refinement of <m> P_{ \epsilon } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        For each index <m> 0 \leq k \lt n </m>, there is some point <m> x_{ k } \leq u_{ k } \leq x_{ k + 1 } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          f \of{ u_{ k } } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } \gt \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } }
        </md>.
        
        If we may choose <m> u_{ k } = x_{ k } </m>, do so. Otherwise, we subdivide <m> \clint{ x_{ k } }{ x_{ k + 1 } } </m> into the union of two closed intervals, one of length
        
        <md>
          L_{ k } \leq \frac{ \epsilon }{ 6 B n }
        </md>
        
        containing <m> x_{ k } </m> and the other of length <m> x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } - L_{ k } </m> containing <m> u_{ k } </m>. Tagging these intervals with <m> x_{ k } </m> and <m> u_{ k } </m>, respectively, we obtain a further refinement <m> \of{ Q_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } \succeq \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , s_{ \epsilon } } </m>. Moreover, we may replace the tagging <m> s_{ \epsilon } </m> with <m> u = \of{ u_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } </m> to obtain a tagged partition <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ x_{ k } } L_{ k } + f \of{ u_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } - L_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \qquad - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ u_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ f \of{ x_{ k } } - f \of{ u_{ k } } } L_{ k } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        In particular,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } } \amp = \abs{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ f \of{ x_{ k } } - f \of{ u_{ k } } } L_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \abs{ f \of{ x_{ k } } - f \of{ u_{ k } } } L_{ k } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \abs{ f \of{ x_{ k } } } + \abs{ f \of{ u_{ k } } } } L_{ k } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \frac{ 2 B \epsilon }{ 6 B n } } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Similarly,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ u_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \gt \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } - \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } - \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } - \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        so that <m> \abs{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } </m>. We conclude that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } - I } \amp \leq \abs{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } } + \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , u } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \qquad + \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ Q_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } - I }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        In particular,
        
        <md>
          U = \inf_{ P }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } } \leq \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ \mathcal{ P }_{ \epsilon } } \lt I + \epsilon
        </md>;
        
        since <m> \epsilon </m> was an arbitrary positive real number, <m> U \leq I </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, now suppose that the nets <m> \mathcal{ U }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> and <m> \mathcal{ L }_{ f } = \of{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } }_{ P } </m> converge upwards to <m> I </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Then there is a partition <m> P_{ \epsilon } = \of{ y_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } - I } , \abs{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } - I } } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        for all refinements <m> P \succeq P_{ \epsilon } </m>. Without loss of generality, we may assume that <m> n \geq 2 </m>. Let
        
        <md>
          \delta = \min \of{ \set{ y_{ k + 1 } - y_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } \cup \set{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 4 \of{ n - 1 } B } , 1 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Suppose that <m> \of{ P , t } </m> is a tagged partition of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> of mesh <m> \mesh \of{ P } \lt \delta </m>, where <m> P = \of{ x_{ j } }_{ j = 0 }^{ m } </m> and <m> t = \of{ t_{ j } }_{ j = 0 }^{ m - 1 } </m>. For each index <m> 0 \leq j \lt m </m>, the corresponding subinterval <m> \clint{ x_{ j } }{ x_{ j + 1 } } </m> either lies entirely inside a subinterval <m> \clint{ y_{ k } }{ y_{ k + 1 } } </m> determined by <m> P_{ \epsilon } </m> or lies in the union <m> \clint{ y_{ k } }{ y_{ k + 2 } } = \clint{ y_{ k } }{ y_{ k + 1 } } \cup \clint{ y_{ k + 1 } }{ y_{ k + 2 } } </m> of two adjacent such subintervals. In particular,
        
        <md>
          x_{ j + 1 } - x_{ j } \leq \mesh \of{ P } \lt \delta \leq \min \of{ \set{ y_{ k + 1 } - y_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } }
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> \clint{ x_{ j } }{ x_{ j + 1 } } </m> cannot span the union of three or more such intervals. Moreover, it cannot occur more than <m> n - 1 </m> times that a subinterval determined by <m> P </m> spans two adjacent subintervals determined by <m> P_{ \epsilon } </m>. 
      </p>
      
      <p>
        We observe that if <m> \clint{ x_{ j } }{ x_{ j + 1 } } \subseteq \clint{ y_{ k } }{ y_{ k + 1 } } </m> for some index <m> 0 \leq k \lt n </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \inf_{ y_{ k } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ j + 1 } - x_{ j } } \leq f \of{ t_{ j } } \of{ x_{ j + 1 } - x_{ j } } \leq \sup_{ y_{ k } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ j + 1 } - x_{ j } }
        </md>.
        
        On the other hand, if otherwise <m> \clint{ x_{ j } }{ x_{ j + 1 } } \subseteq \clint{ y_{ k } }{ y_{ k + 2 } } </m> for some index <m> 0 \leq k \lt n - 1 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          y_{ k } \lt x_{ j } \lt y_{ k + 1 } \lt x_{ j + 1 } \lt y_{ k + 2 }
        </md>.
        
        In this case, if <m> x_{ j } \leq t_{ j } \leq y_{ k + 1 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \inf_{ y_{ k } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - x_{ j } } \leq f \of{ t_{ j } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - x_{ j } } \leq \sup_{ y_{ k } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - x_{ j } }
        </md>,
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ f \of{ t_{ j } } - \sup_{ y_{ k + 1 } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 2 } }{ f \of{ x } } } \of{ x_{ j + 1 } - y_{ k + 1 } } \amp \lt 2 B \delta \leq \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 \of{ n - 1 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \abs{ f \of{ t_{ j } } - \inf_{ y_{ k + 1 } \leq x \leq y_{ k + 2 } }{ f \of{ x } } } \of{ x_{ j + 1 } - y_{ k + 1 } } \amp \lt 2 B \delta \leq \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 \of{ n - 1 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Since this can occur a maximum of <m> n - 1 </m> times, we conclude that
        
        <md>
          I - \epsilon \lt \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } - \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 } \lt \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } \lt \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P_{ \epsilon } } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 } \lt I + \epsilon
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \lt \epsilon </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    The limits of upper and lower Darboux sums are called the <term> upper </term> and <term> lower Darboux integrals </term>. The third condition in <xref ref="theorem-riemann-integrability"/>, that the upper and lower Darboux integrals agree, is sometimes called <term> Darboux integrability </term>. <xref ref="theorem-riemann-integrability"/> shows that Darboux integrability is equivalent to other characterizations of what we call <term> Riemann integrability </term>.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-Riemann-integrability-integral">
    <title>
      Riemann integrability/integral
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      Riemann integrability
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      Riemann integral
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the definite (Riemann) integral of <m> f </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. <m> f </m> is <term> Riemann integrable </term> if there is a real number <m> I \in \R </m> satisfying any (and therefore all) of the equivalent conditions in <xref ref="theorem-riemann-integrability"/>. In this case, that <m> \R </m> is Hausdorff implies that <m> I </m> is uniquely defined by these properties. We call this number
        
        <md>
          I = \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = \inf_{ P }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } } = \sup_{ P }{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } }
        </md>
        
        the <term> (definite) Riemann integral </term> of <m> f </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m>, and we denote it by
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-riemann-integrability">
    <title>
      Riemann integrability
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following are examples and non-examples of Riemann integrable functions and their Riemann integrals:
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> c \in \R </m> be a real number, and consider the constant function <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = c </m>. We observe that
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ c \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = c \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = c \of{ b - a }
                </mrow>
              </md>
              
              for all tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>, where <m> P = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> and <m> t = \of{ t_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } </m>. This implies that
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ c \of{ b - a } } = c \of{ b - a }
              </md>;
              
              that is, <m> f </m> is Riemann integrable, with Riemann integral
              
              <md>
                \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ c \, \diff{ x } } = c \of{ b - a }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> g \colon \clint{ 0 }{ 2 } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined piecewise on the closed interval <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 2 } </m> by the formula
              
              <md>
                g \of{ y } =
                  \begin{cases}
                    1 \amp 0 \leq y \leq 1 \\
                    2 \amp 1 \lt y \leq 2
                  \end{cases}
              </md>.
              
              Let <m> P = \of{ y_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> be a partition of <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 2 } </m>. Then there is a unique index <m> 0 \leq j \lt n </m> so that <m> y_{ j } \leq 1 \lt y_{ j + 1 } </m>. We observe that the restriction of <m> g </m> to every other subinterval of the partition is constant, so that
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \mathcal{ U }_{ g } \of{ P } - \mathcal{ L }_{ g } \of{ P } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \sup_{ y_{ k } \leq y \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - y_{ k } } } - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \inf_{ y_{ k } \leq y \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - y_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \sup_{ y_{ k } \leq y \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } - \inf_{ y_{ k } \leq y \leq y_{ k + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } } \of{ y_{ k + 1 } - y_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \of{ \sup_{ y_{ j } \leq y \leq y_{ j + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } - \inf_{ y_{ j } \leq y \leq y_{ j + 1 } }{ g \of{ y } } } \of{ y_{ j + 1 } - y_{ j } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = 1 \of{ y_{ j + 1 } - y_{ j } } \leq \mesh \of{ P }
                </mrow>
              </md>.
              
              This implies that <m> g </m> is Riemann integrable, although this method does not necessarily directly compute the Riemann integral <m> \int_{ 0 }^{ 2 }{ g \of{ y } \, \diff{ y } } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              More generally, let <m> h \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a non-decreasing real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. We observe that
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \mathcal{ U }_{ g } \of{ P } - \mathcal{ L }_{ g } \of{ P } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \sup_{ z_{ k } \leq z \leq z_{ k + 1 } }{ h \of{ z } } \of{ z_{ k + 1 } - z_{ k } } } - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \inf_{ z_{ k } \leq z \leq z_{ k + 1 } }{ h \of{ z } } \of{ z_{ k + 1 } - z_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ h \of{ z_{ k + 1 } } \of{ z_{ k + 1 } - z_{ k } } } - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ h \of{ z_{ k } } \of{ z_{ k + 1 } - z_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ h \of{ z_{ k + 1 } } - h \of{ z_{ k } } } \of{ z_{ k + 1 } - z_{ k } } } \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ h \of{ z_{ k + 1 } } - h \of{ z_{ k } } } \mesh \of{ P } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \of{ h \of{ b } - h \of{ a } } \mesh \of{ P }
                </mrow>
              </md>
              
              for any partition <m> P = \of{ z_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. This implies that <m> h </m> is Riemann integrable, but the above computation doesn't necessarily aid in the computation of the Riemann integral <m> \int_{ a }^{ b }{ h \of{ z } \, \diff{ z } } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> \chi \colon \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined piecewise by the formula
              
              <md>
                \chi \of{ x } =
                  \begin{cases}
                    1 \amp x \in \Q \\
                    0 \amp x \notin \Q
                  \end{cases}
              </md>.
              
              Since any closed interval contains both rational and irrational numbers,
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \mathcal{ U }_{ \chi } \of{ P } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ \chi \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ 1 \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = 1 - 0 = 1
                </mrow>
              </md>
              
              and
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \mathcal{ L }_{ \chi } \of{ P } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \inf_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ \chi \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
                </mrow>
                
                <mrow>
                  \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ 0 \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = 0
                </mrow>
              </md>
              
              for any partition <m> P = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> of <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m>. We conclude that <m> \chi </m> is not Riemann integrable.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    In general, it can be quite difficult to compute Riemann integrals without additional tools. We will see some such tools in <xref ref="section-the-fundamental-theorems-of-calculus"/>, but even without differential calculus, we may still expand our inventory of Riemann integrable functions significantly. For example, any continuous function defined on a closed interval is Riemann integrable.
  </p>

  <proposition xml:id="proposition-continuity-implies-integrability">
    <title>
      Continuity implies integrability
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. If <m> f </m> is continuous, then it is Riemann integrable.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            U \amp = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } } \amp L \amp = \lim_{ P \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We will show that <m> U \leq L </m>. To that end, the <xref ref="theorem-heine-cantor-theorem" text="title"/> implies that <m> f </m> is uniformly continuous on <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Then there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ b - a }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ U }_{ f } \of{ P } - \mathcal{ L }_{ f } \of{ P } } \amp = \abs{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \inf_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } - \inf_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \abs{ \sup_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } - \inf_{ x_{ k } \leq x \leq x_{ k + 1 } }{ f \of{ x } } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ b - a } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ b - a } } \of{ b - a } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for any partition <m> P = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> of mesh <m> \mesh \of{ P } \lt \delta </m>. In particular, <m> \abs{ U - L } \leq \epsilon </m> for all positive real numbers <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, and so <m> U = L </m>. <xref ref="theorem-riemann-integrability"/> implies that <m> f </m> is Riemann integrable.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    We end this section with a few results about the interplay between Riemann integrals and arithmetic, comparison, and limits. First, the set of Riemann integrable functions is a vector space, and the Riemann integral is a linear functional on this vector space.
  </p>

  <proposition xml:id="proposition-linearity-of-riemann-integration">
    <title>
      Linearity of Riemann integration
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        
        <md>
          f = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } f_{ j } }
        </md>
        
        be a real linear combination of real-valued functions <m> f_{ j } \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. If each <m> f_{ j } </m> is Riemann integrable, then so too is <m> f </m>, and
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f_{ j } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We observe that for each tagged partition <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \of{ \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } f_{ j } \of{ t_{ k } } } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } f_{ j } \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } } = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f_{ j } \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ j } } \of{ P , t } }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        where <m> P = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> and <m> t = \of{ t_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } </m>. Thus
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \d{ x } } \amp = \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ j } } \of{ P , t } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ j } } \of{ P , t } } } = \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ a_{ j } \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f_{ j } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    We emphasize that while it is true that the product of Riemann integrable functions is also Riemann integrable, it is not in general true that the Riemann integral of such a product is the product of the Riemann integrals of the factors; that is,
    
    <md>
      \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } g \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \neq \of{ \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } } \of{ \int_{ a }^{ b }{ g \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } }
    </md>.
    
    You will have an opportunity to explore this further in the problems for this section. First, however, we note that definite integrals preserve comparison of functions.
  </p>

  <lemma xml:id="lemma-riemann-integration-and-comparison">
    <title>
      Riemann integration and comparison
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f , g \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be real-valued functions defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. If <m> f </m> and <m> g </m> are Riemann integrable and <m> f \of{ x } \leq g \of{ x } </m> for all points <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \d{ x } } \leq \int_{ a }^{ b }{ g \of{ x } \, \d{ x } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We observe that for all tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ f \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 }{ g \of{ t_{ k } } \of{ x_{ k + 1 } - x_{ k } } } = \mathcal{ R }_{ g } \of{ P , t }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        where <m> P = \of{ x_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n } </m> and <m> t = \of{ t_{ k } }_{ k = 0 }^{ n - 1 } </m>. Thus
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } \leq \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ g } \of{ P , t } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ g \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <p>
    While the behavior of Riemann integrals under limits can unfortunately be quite subtle, we can establish some preliminary properties. The following theorem establishes the continuity of the Riemann integral on the vector space of Riemann integrable functions on a closed interval, taken with the compact open topology:
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-uniform-convergence-and-riemann-integrability">
    <title>
      Uniform convergence and Riemann integrability
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a uniformly convergent sequence of real-valued functions <m> f_{ n } \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. If each <m> f_{ n } </m> is Riemann integrable, then the uniform limit <m> \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ f_{ n } } </m> is Riemann integrable, with Riemann integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ f_{ n } } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f_{ n } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We first argue that the sequence <m> \of{ I_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of Riemann integrals
        
        <md>
          I_{ n } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f_{ n } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </md>
        
        is Cauchy. To that end, let <m> f = \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ f_{ n } } </m>. Since <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> f </m>, there is some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ f_{ n } \of{ x } - f \of{ x } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 6 \of{ b - a } }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ f_{ m } \of{ x } - f_{ n } \of{ x } } \amp \leq \abs{ f_{ m } \of{ x } - f \of{ x } } + \abs{ f_{ n } \of{ x } - f \of{ x } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 6 \of{ b - a } } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 6 \of{ b - a } } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m , n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ m } } \of{ P , t } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } } \amp = \abs{ \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ f_{ m } \of{ t_{ i } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } } - \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ f_{ n } \of{ t_{ i } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ \abs{ f_{ m } \of{ t_{ i } } - f_{ n } \of{ t_{ i } } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ b - a } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> m , n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>, where <m> P = \of{ x_{ i } }_{ i = 0 }^{ l } </m> and <m> t = \of{ t_{ i } }_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ P , t } </m> be a tagged partition of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ m } } \of{ P , t } - I_{ m } } \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } - I_{ n } } \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Thus
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ I_{ m } - I_{ n } } \amp \leq \abs{ I_{ m } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ m } } \of{ P , t } } + \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ m } } \of{ P , t } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } } + \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } - I_{ n } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We conclude that <m> \of{ I_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is Cauchy.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        <xref ref="corollary-completeness-of-the-real-line"/> implies that this sequence converges to a real number <m> I \in \R </m>. We will show that
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ \of{ P , t } \uparrow }{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } } = I
        </md>.
        
        To that end, let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Since <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> f </m>, there is some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ f_{ n } \of{ x } - f \of{ x } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } } \amp = \abs{ \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ f \of{ t_{ i } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } } - \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ f_{ n } \of{ t_{ i } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ \abs{ f_{ n } \of{ t_{ i } } - f \of{ t_{ i } } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \sum_{ i = 0 }^{ l - 1 }{ \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ x_{ i + 1 } - x_{ i } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \of{ b - a } } } \of{ b - a } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and tagged partitions <m> \of{ P , t } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. Since <m> \of{ I_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> I </m>, there is a natural number <m> m_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ I_{ n } - I } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq m_{ \epsilon } </m>. Fix <m> n \geq \max \of{ \set{ m_{ \epsilon } , n_{ \epsilon } } } </m>. Then there is some tagged partition <m> \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m> of <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } - I_{ n } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
        </md>
        
        for all refinements <m> \of{ P , t } \succeq \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - I } \amp \leq \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } - \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } } + \abs{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f_{ n } } \of{ P , t } - I_{ n } } + \abs{ I_{ n } - I }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all refinements <m> \of{ P , t } \succeq \of{ P_{ \epsilon } , t_{ \epsilon } } </m>. Thus the net <m> \of{ \mathcal{ R }_{ f } \of{ P , t } }_{ \of{ P , t } } </m> converges to <m> I </m>, and so <m> f </m> is Riemann integrable, with Riemann integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = I = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ I_{ n } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f_{ n } \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      We will see in <xref ref="section-improper-integration"/> that an analogue <xref ref="theorem-uniform-convergence-and-riemann-integrability"/> does not hold for improper integration over non-compact intervals.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
